    "narrative": "The slate for May 7th heavily favors our outfield trio, led by **Corbin Carroll** (79.72) and **James Wood** (78.41). **Corbin Carroll** is our highest-rated play, benefiting from a soft matchup against Mitch Keller; the dynamic platoon advantage and favorable spot in the three-hole create an ideal ceiling for stolen base production, our highest priority. Meanwhile, **James Wood** is in a prime position to capitalize on a weak matchup against Simeon Woods Richardson. With a 0.378 xwOBA and the leadoff slot providing maximum plate appearance volume, he is essentially a lock for an elite outing. **Mickey Moniak** rounds out this group with a 75.75 score, bolstered significantly by a massive 14% park factor boost and his promotion to the two-hole against Scott.\n\nMid-tier decisions were defined by the marginal starts of **Trevor Story** and **Kyle Karros**. While **Trevor Story** faces a tougher arm in Jax (3.02 xERA), his inclusion is driven by the \"Slow SP Delivery\" metric, which gives him a calculated 4% boost to his stolen base probability\u2014an essential nuance given our roster construction. **Kyle Karros** holds onto his spot primarily due to a 14% park factor uplift, though we are closely monitoring his production as he hits toward the bottom of the order. The most difficult bench decision involved **Jose Caballero**; while his 13% platoon boost against left-hander Gore made him mathematically intriguing, his lack of confirmation in the official lineup, combined with a negative adjustment for facing a pitcher who effectively controls the running game, forced him to the bench to preserve our precious games-played cap.\n\nWe are operating with a relatively clean bill of health, though we continue to monitor the status of **Giancarlo Stanton**, who remains on the IL, and ensure that our depth pieces like **Brice Turang**, **Gabriel Moreno**, and **Max Muncy** stay prepared for rotation shifts. Today\u2019s lineup is intentionally aggressive; by benching the inactive **Josh Naylor** and **Austin Riley**\u2014both of whom are currently sitting on team off-days\u2014we are maximizing the efficiency of our active spots. Keep a close watch on **Tyler Soderstrom**; his 67.16 score is robust, but he is facing Painter, a solid arm that warrants a final check on weather conditions before first pitch to ensure no wind shifts negate his park advantage.",
